import{_ as a,o as t,c as r,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.oeh4wjV1.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Two-Packet Jumpstart","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"formats/jumpstart/variants/two-packet.md","filePath":"formats/jumpstart/variants/two-packet.md","lastUpdated":1792425987000}'),o={name:"formats/jumpstart/variants/two-packet.md"};function n(i,e,l,c,d,h){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="two-packet-jumpstart" tabindex="-1">Two-Packet Jumpstart <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-packet-jumpstart" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two-Packet Jumpstart&quot;">​</a></h1><p><a href="./../">Jumpstart parent page</a> · <strong>Rules: two prepared packets per player, using ordinary two-player Magic</strong></p><h2 id="players-and-materials" tabindex="-1">Players and materials <a class="header-anchor" href="#players-and-materials" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Players and materials&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This ruleset is for <strong>two players</strong>. Each player needs <strong>two Jumpstart packets</strong>, for four packets total. Each packet contains <strong>twenty playable cards, including its lands</strong>. Theme cards, packet dividers, tokens, and ordinary reminder inserts are kept separately and do not count toward those twenty cards.</p><p>Supply life counters and any tokens, counters, or other components required by the cards. Use official packets or a prepared custom box with complete twenty-card packet lists. A custom packet is built before the play session using the <a href="./../building-packets.html">packet-building guide</a>.</p><p>Jumpstart&#39;s deck-assembly procedure comes from the official product. The opening-hand and game procedures below use ordinary two-player Magic. If an event or box specifies a different procedure, such as a free first mulligan, that is an explicit addition to this version.</p><h2 id="select-packets" tabindex="-1">Select packets <a class="header-anchor" href="#select-packets" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Select packets&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Identify the available packet pool and read any box-specific pairing restrictions before distributing packets.</li><li>Give each player two complete packets. With unopened retail boosters, each player opens their two boosters. With a reusable box, use the box&#39;s stated selection method. If it has none, shuffle its packet-divider cards face down and deal two to each player, then take the matching packets.</li><li>Set aside the theme cards, dividers, tokens, and other non-deck inserts.</li><li>Each player may inspect their own two packets before combining them. A known packet list does not reveal the order of the shuffled deck.</li></ol><p>The face-down divider deal is this catalogue&#39;s default for distributing a reusable collection; buying random boosters is not required. A box may instead allow players to choose themes or draft whole packets, but it must supply that selection procedure. The unselected packets remain outside the game.</p><h2 id="assemble-the-decks" tabindex="-1">Assemble the decks <a class="header-anchor" href="#assemble-the-decks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Assemble the decks&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Combine <strong>all twenty cards from each selected packet</strong> into a single <strong>forty-card deck</strong>.</li><li>Keep the lands that came with both packets. There is no separate land-adding or card-cutting step.</li><li>Keep each player&#39;s deck, library, hand, graveyard, and other zones separate from their opponent&#39;s.</li></ol><p>The combined deck is not a pool from which to choose a smaller deck. Do not replace cards from unused packets or a personal collection during the game. This setup supplies no sideboard or extra companion card.</p><p>Two packets of the same color or theme can be combined. Copies of a card supplied by the two packets remain in the deck; Jumpstart does not impose singleton or the constructed four-copy restriction on the contents of those packets. This catalogue&#39;s preference to design custom packets with few duplicates is a preparation choice, not a rule that removes cards from official packets.</p><p>Cards from different Jumpstart releases can be combined. Custom boxes can define their own card pools, but should state any restrictions before players select packets. A card&#39;s legality in Standard or Commander is not an additional restriction on this Jumpstart game.</p><h2 id="set-up-the-game" tabindex="-1">Set up the game <a class="header-anchor" href="#set-up-the-game" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Set up the game&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Randomly determine who chooses which player takes the first turn, using a die roll, coin flip, or another agreed method.</li><li>Each player starts at <strong>20 life</strong>.</li><li>Each player thoroughly shuffles their combined deck and offers it to their opponent to cut or shuffle. It becomes their library.</li><li>Each player draws <strong>seven cards</strong>.</li><li>Resolve mulligans as described below.</li><li>Resolve any actions a card permits from an opening hand, in starting-player order, then begin the first turn.</li></ol><h3 id="mulligans" tabindex="-1">Mulligans <a class="header-anchor" href="#mulligans" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mulligans&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Use the normal London mulligan for a two-player game:</p><ol><li>Starting with the player taking the first turn, each player declares whether to keep their hand or take a mulligan.</li><li>Each player taking a mulligan shuffles their hand back into their library and draws seven cards again.</li><li>That player puts a number of cards equal to their total number of mulligans on the bottom of their library in any order: one after their first mulligan, two after their second, and so on.</li><li>Repeat for players who still want to mulligan. A player who keeps their hand takes no further mulligans. Stop when everyone has kept, or a player&#39;s opening hand would be zero cards.</li></ol><p>This version has <strong>no free first mulligan</strong>. A mulligan changes the opening hand, not the selected packets or deck contents.</p><h2 id="turns-spells-and-combat" tabindex="-1">Turns, spells, and combat <a class="header-anchor" href="#turns-spells-and-combat" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Turns, spells, and combat&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The starting player <strong>skips the draw step of their first turn</strong>. After that exception, players alternate ordinary Magic turns, with the normal beginning phase, first main phase, combat phase, second main phase, and ending phase.</p><p>Use normal land-play limits, mana costs, spell timing, priority, combat, and card text. Jumpstart supplies no extra mana, shared resources, free spells, or special combat procedure. The <a href="./../../../rules/">shared rules reference</a> explains the underlying Magic rules assumed by the catalogue.</p><p>Legendary creatures are shuffled into the deck like other cards. They do not become commanders. There is no commander selection, command-zone casting, commander tax, commander damage, or Commander color-identity restriction. Ordinary rules such as the legend rule still apply.</p><p>Packet labels and sleeve stickers are sorting aids. They do not change a card&#39;s name, color, types, abilities, or ownership, and they do not authorize looking at a hidden card. A thematic character or team label does not grant an ability that is absent from the actual card.</p><h2 id="winning-and-losing" tabindex="-1">Winning and losing <a class="header-anchor" href="#winning-and-losing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Winning and losing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Normal Magic victory and loss conditions apply. A player normally loses when they have <strong>zero or less life</strong>, have <strong>ten or more poison counters</strong>, attempt to draw more cards than remain in their library, or are made to lose by a card effect. They may also concede. Merely having an empty library does not cause a loss until a required draw cannot be completed.</p><p>When one player loses, the other wins. Card effects can provide other ways to win or modify a loss condition. If both players lose simultaneously, the game is a draw. Jumpstart adds no separate scoring, boss objective, or alternate victory condition.</p><h2 id="another-game" tabindex="-1">Another game <a class="header-anchor" href="#another-game" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Another game&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The procedure above plays a single game. Players may shuffle the same pair of packets for a rematch or reset the packets and select new combinations.</p><p>If playing a match with the same decks, agree on its length before starting. The loser of the previous game chooses who takes the first turn in the next game. After a drawn game, the player who made that choice previously makes it again. Keep the selected forty-card decks intact between games unless the box explicitly defines a different match structure.</p><h2 id="reset-a-reusable-box" tabindex="-1">Reset a reusable box <a class="header-anchor" href="#reset-a-reusable-box" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reset a reusable box&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Collect cards from every zone, including cards that changed controllers, were exiled, or were set aside to represent another card. Restore double-faced cards to their front faces for sorting.</li><li>Remove tokens, counters, and other non-deck components.</li><li>Sort the cards face up by their packet markers, including the basic lands. Each physical packet has its own identifier.</li><li>Count <strong>twenty cards per packet</strong>, then return each packet to its matching divider or container.</li><li>If a count is wrong or a marker is missing, check that packet&#39;s stored list before returning it to the available pool.</li></ol><p>The <a href="./../building-packets.html#double-sleeve-and-mark-every-card">preparation guide</a> explains how to protect the markers inside sleeves. Its decklists are a reference for missing cards and changes; ordinary cleanup uses the markers and a card count.</p><h2 id="sources" tabindex="-1">Sources <a class="header-anchor" href="#sources" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sources&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="https://magic.wizards.com/en/products/jumpstart" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Wizards&#39; Jumpstart overview and FAQ</a>: two themed twenty-card packets, lands included, combined into one forty-card deck.</li><li><a href="https://magic.wizards.com/en/news/announcements/introducing-jumpstart-new-way-play-magic-2020-02-20" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Introducing Jumpstart</a>: the original packet-based play structure.</li><li><a href="https://magic.wizards.com/en/news/feature/jumpstart-decklists-2020-06-18" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Official Jumpstart decklists</a>: examples of complete packet contents and multiple versions of a theme.</li><li><a href="https://magic.wizards.com/en/rules" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Magic Comprehensive Rules</a>, especially 100.2b, 103, and 104: Limited deck construction, starting the game, mulligans, and game-ending conditions.</li></ul><p>The reusable-box distribution fallback and packet-reset procedure are catalogue conventions. The <a href="./../building-packets.html">custom-packet guide</a> records the owner&#39;s preparation preferences; they are not requirements imposed on every official Jumpstart product.</p>',37)])])}const u=a(o,[["render",n]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
